  "content": {
    "title": "Breaking down Dan Hurley and the UConn men's basketball coaching staff",
    "text": "CINCINNATI, OH - JANUARY 25: Connecticut Huskies head coach Dan Hurley and associate head coach Kimani Young react during a college basketball game against the Xavier Musketeers on Jan. 25, 2025 at Cintas Center in Cincinnati, Ohio. (Photo by Joe Robbins/Icon Sportswire via Getty Images)Icon Sportswire/Icon Sportswire via Getty ImagesThe UConn men's basketball team is heading to the Final Four for the third time in the last four years, hoping to play for its third NCAA title in that same timeframe. Led by Dan Hurley, who often boasts of having the best coaching staff in the country, this group of coaches has helped justify the claim that Storrs is the basketball capital of the world. Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢Here is a look at the UConn's coaching staff:Dan HurleyWASHINGTON, DC - MARCH 29: Head coach Dan Hurley of the UConn Huskies cuts down the net after defeating the Duke Blue Devils 73-72 in the Elite Eight of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament at Capital One Arena on March 29, 2026 in Washington, DC. (Photo by Patrick Smith/Getty Images)Patrick Smith/Getty ImagesHurley was hired by UConn on March 22, 2018 from the University of Rhode Island.Article continues below this adIn his first seven years in Storrs, Hurley brought the UConn program back to familiar heights, claiming back-to-back NCAA national titles in 2023 and 2024. Make CT Insider a preferred source on Google to see more of our journalism when you search.Add Preferred SourceHurley began his college coaching career in 2010 at Wagner University (2010-12) before heading to Rhode Island (2012-18). Since arriving at UConn, Hurley has steadily improved the program. In his first year the team had a 16-17 record, which improved to 19-12 a year later (2019-20). During the 2020-21 season UConn went 15-8 and then improved to 23-10 a year later. Article continues below this adDuring the 2022-23 season, UConn went 31-8 and won a National Title. During the 2023-24 season, Hurley led UConn to its highest win total in program history going 37-3 and claiming a second straight NCAA championship. In 2024 Hurley became the first UConn men's coach to win Naismith College Coach of the Year. Hurley was born and raised in Jersey City, and is the son of Hall of Fame high school coach Bob Hurley Sr. He played for his father at St. Anthony with his older brother Bobby, who later starred at Duke and played in the NBA.After playing at Seton Hall, Hurley moved into coaching as an assistant under his father, then moved on to Rutgers before leading St. Benedict's Prep in Newark for a decade.Article continues below this adKimani YoungCHICAGO, ILLINOIS - DECEMBER 21: Silas Demary Jr.#2, Associate Head Coach Kimani Young, Malachi Smith #0, Alex Karaban #11, Jayden Ross #23 and Tarris Reed Jr.#5 of the UConn Huskies look on during the first half of the game against the DePaul Blue Demons at Wintrust Arena on December 21, 2025 in Chicago, Illinois. (Photo by Harry Figiel/ISI Photos/ISI Photos via Getty Images)Harry Figiel/ISI Photos/ISI Photos via Getty ImagesThe New York native joined the UConn coaching staff when Hurley began, and was promoted to associate head coach in September 2020. Young is credited as being a dynamic coach and recruiter.Young played at Texas El Paso for Don Haskins and worked in youth basketball in New York before moving into college coaching. He started at Florida International under Richard Pitino and was on Pitino's Minnesota staff for five years before joining UConn.Article continues below this adYoung is respected in the coaching world and his name has been floated as a candidate for head coaching positions, including UMass in 2022. He is involved in all aspects of the program, but has a focus on guard play. His guidance has helped with the success of such guards as James Bouknight, R.J. Cole, Tyrese Martin, Jordan Hawkins, Cam Spencer, Stephon Castle and Tristen Newton. Luke MurrayWASHINGTON, DC - MARCH 29: UConn assistant coach Luke Murray looks on prior to a game against the Duke Blue Devils in the Elite Eight of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament at Capital One Arena on March 29, 2026 in Washington, DC. (Photo by Patrick Smith/Getty Images)Patrick Smith/Getty ImagesThe son of actor Bill Murray grew up in New York, but he attended St. Luke's in New Canaan and spent his freshman year of college at UConn before graduating from Fairfield University.Article continues below this adHe began coaching at Quinnipiac under Tom Moore (UConn's current general manager) and was at Post University before eventually working under Hurley at Wagner and Rhode Island. He has also held positions at Arizona, Towson, Xavier and Louisville.Often considered one of the hot coaching prospects in the game, Murray accepted the head coaching position at Boston College on March 26. He will remain with UConn until the end of its season.Murray was hired by UConn in April 2021. He is credited with helping install a complex, high-motion and highly-successful offensive system with the Huskies that led to back-to-back national championships in 2023 and 2024. Murray has also been instrumental in the recruitment of numerous UConn players, including current NBAers Donovan Clingan and Liam McNeeley and current Husky freshman Braylon Mullins out of high school and current Memphis Grizzly Cam Spencer out of the portal.Mike NardiUConn Assistant Coach Mike Nardi during an NCAA men's college basketball exhibition game against Boston College at Mohegan Sun Arena in Uncasville, Conn., Monday, October 13, 2025.Dave Zajac/Hearst Connecticut MediaThe newest member of the UConn coaching staff joined the team in April, 2025.Article continues below this adNardi had spent the prior 10 seasons with the Villanova coaching staff (his alma mater) following an eight year professional career playing overseas in Italy and the Netherlands.He was a part of two national championships (2016 and 2018) and three Final Fours (2016, 2018 and 2022) while at Villanova. Score exclusive insights and analysis on UConn athletics.April 1, 2026Will Aldam has been a sports reporter with Hearst Connecticut Media since 2018.",
